            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Heaven is primarily understood as the transcendent dwelling place and throne of God, as well as the eternal destination and home where the redeemed go to dwell with Him.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Heaven is defined jurisdictionally as the realm where God's will is already done; its eschatological direction is downward to earth, explicitly not an upward destination for humanity.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God's transcendent dwelling place, the center of divine authority, and the heavenly home of believers.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The jurisdiction of God's rule and visible sky, characterized by downward movement to earth.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book excludes the concept of believers 'going up' to heaven, restricting the symbol to God's jurisdiction that ultimately descends to earth.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly denies that heaven is an upward destination for humanity ('not man gone up'), contradicting the part of the primary consensus that views heaven as the eternal home believers depart to upon death.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book retains the primary consensus identification of heaven as God's seat of rule and dwelling (a core shared across traditions). However, it refines the scope by strictly emphasizing its jurisdictional nature and downward motion, selecting a recognized biblical-theological alternative that rejects the popular idea of heaven as a postmortem upward destination.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The New Testament also speaks of believers having an inheritance kept in heaven (1 Peter 1:4), a citizenship in heaven (Philippians 3:20), and the desire to depart and be with Christ (Philippians 1:23), which may complicate the absolute denial of any 'upward' translation of the believer.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
